  "articleBody": "If your bathroom door frame is constantly swelling, it's almost always a sign of a moisture problem. Bathrooms are inherently humid environments, but persistent swelling means water is getting into the frame where it shouldn't be. One of the most common culprits is inadequate ventilation. If you're not running an exhaust fan during and after showers, that steam has to go somewhere, and often it gets absorbed by porous materials like wood door frames. Over time, this repeated absorption causes the wood to expand and contract, leading to swelling, cracking, and even rot. Another frequent issue is direct water exposure. Splashes from the shower or tub, or even water on the floor that isn't quickly cleaned up, can seep into the bottom of the door frame. If the frame isn't properly sealed or if the flooring around it isn't waterproofed correctly, it's an open invitation for moisture. Sometimes, the problem could also be a leak in a nearby pipe or even an issue with the sealing around the tub or shower that's allowing water to get to the wall cavity behind the frame. It's a mistake to just patch the surface damage without finding and fixing the source of the moisture. Ignoring the root cause means the swelling will just come back. HH Remodeling LLC can help you identify why your door frame is swelling, address the underlying moisture issues, and perform lasting repairs so your door operates smoothly again.",
